registerCourse({
  code: "CCAR-F",
  name: "Claude Certified Architect",
  tier: "Foundations",
  blurb: "Scenario-based architecture judgement: agentic loops and orchestration, tool and MCP design, Claude Code configuration, prompting, and context reliability.",
  audience: "Solution architects with hands-on experience designing systems on Claude and the Anthropic API.",
  items: 60,
  minutes: 120,
  passScore: 720,
  fee: "$125 USD",
  domains: [
    { id: "R1", name: "Agentic Architecture", weight: 27.0 },
    { id: "R3", name: "Claude Code & Workflows", weight: 20.0 },
    { id: "R4", name: "Prompt Engineering & Output", weight: 20.0 },
    { id: "R2", name: "Tool Design & MCP", weight: 18.0 },
    { id: "R5", name: "Context & Reliability", weight: 15.0 },
  ],
  scenarioDraw: { scenarios: 4, perScenario: 15 },
  scenarios: [
    { id: "support", title: "Customer Support Resolution Agent", text: "You are building a customer support resolution agent with the Claude Agent SDK. It handles high-ambiguity requests such as returns, billing disputes, and account changes. It has tools for order lookup, refund issuing, and ticket escalation, and it reads from a policy knowledge base. Conversations can run long, and some cases must reach a human." },
    { id: "codegen", title: "Code Generation with Claude Code", text: "Your team uses Claude Code for generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation across a large shared repository. Engineers work in different subdirectories with different conventions. You are responsible for configuring it so the whole team gets consistent behaviour, and for deciding what belongs in shared configuration versus each developer's own setup." },
    { id: "research", title: "Multi-Agent Research System", text: "You are building a multi-agent research system on the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent receives a research question, decomposes it, and delegates to subagents that search and read sources. Subagents return findings the coordinator synthesises into a report. Runs are long, sources are numerous, and the report must be traceable back to what was actually found." },
    { id: "devtools", title: "Developer Productivity Tooling", text: "You are building internal tooling on the Claude Agent SDK to help engineers explore unfamiliar codebases, understand legacy systems, and answer questions about internal services. It connects to several internal systems through MCP servers, and different teams need access to different subsets of those systems." },
    { id: "cicd", title: "Claude Code in a CI/CD Pipeline", text: "You are integrating Claude Code into a CI/CD pipeline that runs automated code review on pull requests, generates release notes, and triages failing builds. It runs unattended with no interactive terminal, its output is consumed by other pipeline steps, and a failure must not silently block the team's merges." },
    { id: "extract", title: "Structured Data Extraction", text: "You are building a system that extracts structured information from unstructured documents — invoices, contracts, and scanned forms — validates it against a schema, and writes it to a downstream database. Documents vary in quality and completeness, and incorrect data reaching the database is expensive to unwind." },
  ],
  questions: [
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent sometimes keeps working after the user's problem is resolved. What should determine when its loop ends?",o:["A phrase in the response text indicating the work is finished","A timer that ends the loop after a set number of seconds","A fixed cap on how many iterations the loop may run for","The stop reason returned with the model's response"],c:[3],e:"Loop termination should be driven by the structured stop reason. Parsing natural language for completion signals is brittle, and caps and timers are safety nets rather than the primary mechanism.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A refund tool must never run before an eligibility check. What is the reliable way to enforce that order?",o:["A tool description on the refund tool noting the check comes first","An example in the prompt showing the correct order being followed","A programmatic hook that blocks the refund call until the check has run","A system prompt instruction stating the required order of the two tools"],c:[2],e:"Financial ordering constraints need programmatic enforcement. Prompt instructions, descriptions, and examples all have a non-zero failure rate that a business rule cannot tolerate.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Which condition is a sound trigger for escalating a support case to a human?",o:["The customer's messages indicate they are becoming frustrated","The conversation has exceeded a set number of exchanged turns","The agent reports low confidence in the answer it has produced","The policy knowledge base does not cover the situation presented"],c:[3],e:"A genuine policy gap is a reliable, checkable trigger. Detected sentiment and self-reported confidence are unreliable signals, and turn count doesn't track difficulty.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Long conversations are summarised to save context, and order numbers are being lost. What is the fix?",o:["Summarise less aggressively so more of the original text survives","Hold transactional details in a facts block that is never summarised","Ask the model to preserve any numbers it encounters when summarising","Move to a model with a larger context window so summarising is rarer"],c:[1],e:"Progressive summarisation paraphrases exact values away. A verbatim facts block carried in every prompt is what protects transactional specifics.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The agent frequently calls the ticket escalation tool when it should call order lookup. What should be fixed first?",o:["The two tool descriptions, so their boundaries are unambiguous","The system prompt, by adding rules about which tool to prefer","The tool set, by merging the two tools into a single broader one","The model tier, by moving to one better at selecting between tools"],c:[0],e:"Misrouting between similar tools is a description problem first. Clarifying what each does and where the boundary lies precedes prompt rules or restructuring.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"What should a strong description for the refund tool specify?",o:["A warning that refunds are a sensitive financial operation","What it does, its expected inputs, and when not to use it","The internal service it calls and the team that maintains it","The average time the underlying refund operation takes to run"],c:[1],e:"Purpose, inputs, and explicit boundaries against similar tools are what drive correct selection. Implementation detail and generic warnings don't help the model choose.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent has been given all fourteen available tools. What is the likely consequence?",o:["Response latency rises in proportion to the number of tools defined","The agent refuses to act when too many tools are available at once","Selection accuracy degrades as the number of similar options grows","Tool definitions crowd out the conversation history in the context"],c:[2],e:"Scoping an agent to the tools its role needs is what preserves selection reliability; a large undifferentiated tool set is the main cause of misselection.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An order lookup tool fails mid-conversation. What should the agent do?",o:["End the conversation and hand the customer to a human immediately","Answer as though the lookup succeeded, using its best guess at the data","Retry the lookup until it succeeds before continuing the conversation","Continue, state clearly what could not be retrieved"],c:[3],e:"Graceful degradation with a visible annotation is the pattern. Silent failure and hard termination are both wrong, and unbounded retries stall the interaction.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Which part of the agent should own the decision to escalate?",o:["The agent loop, using defined criteria checked on each turn","The refund tool, which can escalate when a case looks unusual","The customer, who can request a human at any point in the conversation","The knowledge base, which flags topics that require human handling"],c:[0],e:"Escalation is a control-flow decision belonging to the loop against explicit criteria. A customer request is one such criterion but not the whole mechanism.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The agent gives inconsistent answers to the same policy question across conversations. What is the most likely cause?",o:["The model is inherently inconsistent on questions of this kind","The policy knowledge base contains too few documents overall","The relevant policy is not being reliably retrieved into context","The conversations are too short for the policy to take effect"],c:[2],e:"Inconsistency on a documented policy usually means retrieval isn't reliably surfacing the governing passage, so the model is answering from general knowledge instead.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A new tool is needed to check warranty status against a third-party system. What should be evaluated first?",o:["Which model tier will call the new tool most reliably","Whether an existing MCP server already exposes that system","How the new tool's description ought to be worded for the model","Which transport the new custom server ought to use"],c:[1],e:"Checking for an existing server before building one avoids duplicating an integration that may already be maintained elsewhere.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent occasionally issues a refund larger than the original order. What control addresses this most directly?",o:["Validation inside the refund tool that rejects amounts above the order total","A note in the tool's description about the maximum refund permitted","A review of refunds above the order total after they have been issued","A prompt instruction never to refund more than the original order was worth"],c:[0],e:"A hard constraint belongs in the tool's own validation, where it cannot be talked around. Prompt-level rules and after-the-fact review both allow the bad call through.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Key case details appear in the middle of a very long prompt and are being missed. What is the practical fix?",o:["Move the critical details to the start of the prompt where they hold attention","Ask the model to read the whole of the prompt carefully before answering","Shorten every other part of the prompt so that the details stand out more","Repeat the case details several times throughout the whole body of the prompt"],c:[0],e:"Content buried mid-prompt is the weakest position. Relocating the material that matters to the front is the direct remedy for the lost-in-the-middle effect.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Which tool_choice setting forces the agent to call some tool but lets it pick which?",o:["any","none","a specific named tool","auto"],c:[0],e:"'any' requires a tool call while leaving the selection to the model; 'auto' permits a text-only reply, and naming a tool removes the choice entirely.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The team wants to add a second agent that reviews the first agent's proposed refunds. What makes this effective?",o:["The reviewer uses a faster model tier so the added step stays cheap","The reviewer runs with its own context and a distinct reviewing brief","The reviewer is the same agent asked to re-examine its own decision","The reviewer sees only the refund amount without the surrounding case"],c:[1],e:"Independent review needs separate context and a genuinely different brief. Self-review inherits the reasoning that produced the decision in the first place.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"What is the risk of letting the agent's conversation history grow without management?",o:["The conversation becomes unrecoverable if the session is interrupted","Tool definitions are dropped once the history passes a certain size","The agent begins repeating its earlier responses back to the customer","Early instructions lose influence as later content accumulates"],c:[3],e:"Unmanaged growth dilutes the instructions that were set at the outset, which is why long-running agents need active context management.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The refund tool and a separate 'goodwill credit' tool have nearly identical descriptions. What follows?",o:["The agent will always choose whichever tool is defined first","The API will reject the request for containing ambiguous tools","The agent will route between them unpredictably","The agent will call both tools and let the application decide"],c:[2],e:"Near-identical descriptions leave the model without a basis for choosing, which shows up as unpredictable routing rather than a defined fallback or an error.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"m",q:"Which two belong in the agent loop rather than in the prompt? (Select 2)",o:["Checking the stop reason to decide whether to continue","Explaining what the company's returns window is","Describing the tone the agent should use with customers","Enforcing that eligibility is verified before a refund runs"],c:[0,3],e:"Control flow and hard business constraints belong in code; tone and policy content are prompt and knowledge concerns.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A case is escalated to a human. What should be passed along with it?",o:["The agent's confidence score for the answer it could not give","What was attempted, what was established","The full untrimmed transcript of the entire agent conversation","A one-line summary of the customer's original opening message"],c:[1],e:"A structured handover of attempts, findings, and the escalation reason is what makes the human's job possible without re-reading everything.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Why should the order lookup tool return a structured result rather than free text?",o:["Free text cannot be returned from a tool under the Messages API","Structured results are processed by a different, faster code path","Free text results are not retained in the conversation history","Structured fields are checkable and consume less context than prose"],c:[3],e:"Structured tool results are both cheaper in context and verifiable downstream, which matters when the value drives a financial decision.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Team-wide coding standards keep failing to reach new joiners. Where should they live?",o:["In a wiki page that new joiners are asked to read during onboarding","In each developer's user-level configuration on their own machine","In project-level configuration committed to the repository","In the prompt each developer writes at the start of a session"],c:[2],e:"User-level config stays on one machine and never reaches anyone else. Project-level configuration travels with the repository, which is what makes standards shared.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Test conventions need to apply across many directories that don't share a parent. What fits best?",o:["A directory-level configuration file placed in each affected folder","A single root-level file listing every directory the rules apply to","Path-specific rules matched by glob patterns across the codebase","A user-level file maintained by whoever owns the test suite"],c:[2],e:"Glob-matched rules apply across a codebase regardless of location, whereas directory-level files are bound to where they sit.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An engineer is starting a multi-file migration with an unclear approach. What should they use?",o:["A single very detailed prompt describing every edit in advance","Direct execution, since the migration will need many edits anyway","A separate session for each file so the changes stay independent","Plan mode, to explore the approach before any changes are committed"],c:[3],e:"Plan mode suits work where the right approach needs establishing first; direct execution fits clear, contained tasks.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"What belongs in project configuration rather than in a Skill?",o:["Instructions for a workflow used by only one team member","The procedure for a specific recurring task like a release","A step-by-step guide for generating a particular report","Standards that apply to all work in the repository"],c:[3],e:"Universal standards belong in configuration; specific repeatable procedures are what Skills package.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A code review prompt says 'be conservative about flagging issues' and results are inconsistent. What is the fix?",o:["Instruct it to flag fewer issues than it did on the previous run","Move to a more capable model tier to improve its judgement","Ask for a confidence score attached to each issue that is raised","Replace it with categorical criteria stating exactly what to flag"],c:[3],e:"Vague confidence language isn't actionable. Explicit categorical criteria — what counts as an issue — is what produces consistency.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated documentation is inconsistent in structure despite detailed written instructions. What usually helps more?",o:["Two or three concrete examples showing the intended output","A longer and more precise set of written instructions","A rule forbidding any deviation from the described structure","A request to re-read the instructions before generating"],c:[0],e:"Concrete examples anchor format more reliably than adding further prose instruction, which is a well-established pattern in prompt design.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A Skill produces very verbose intermediate output that clutters the main session. What addresses this?",o:["Running the Skill in an isolated sub-agent context","Asking the Skill to produce less intermediate output","Truncating the Skill's output before it reaches the session","Running the Skill in a separate terminal session entirely"],c:[0],e:"Context isolation keeps a verbose Skill's working output out of the main conversation while still returning its result.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An engineer's session has drifted and Claude is applying conventions from an unrelated part of the repo. What is the cause?",o:["The repository's configuration files conflict with one another","The engineer's user-level configuration has overridden the project's","Accumulated context from earlier work is still influencing the session","The model has been switched mid-session without the engineer noticing"],c:[2],e:"Carried-over context from earlier tasks is the usual cause of convention bleed, which is why a fresh session helps.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Which tool should be used to find files whose contents match a pattern?",o:["A content search tool such as grep","A filename matching tool such as glob","A general-purpose file reading tool","A directory listing of the repository root"],c:[0],e:"Content search and filename matching are distinct; using the filename tool to search contents is a common and avoidable mistake.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Two configuration files apply to the same file being edited. How should this be designed?",o:["With whichever configuration was modified most recently taking effect","With only the most specific configuration applying and the other ignored","With the two merged into a single file to avoid the overlap entirely","With the more specific configuration layering on top of the broader one"],c:[3],e:"Cascading configuration is the intended model: broader guidance applies, with more specific guidance refining it closer to the code.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated code passes review but repeatedly misses an internal library the team prefers. What should change?",o:["Each engineer should mention the library in their own individual prompts","The project configuration should name the preferred library and when to use it","The library should be renamed so that it is easier for the model to find","Review should be tightened so that the omission gets caught before any merge"],c:[1],e:"A durable team preference belongs in shared configuration rather than depending on each engineer remembering to say it.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long refactoring session is losing track of decisions made an hour earlier. What is the practical remedy?",o:["Continue and re-explain each decision whenever it becomes relevant","Capture the decisions in a persistent form the session can re-read","Break the refactor into files handled in completely separate sessions","Ask the model to recall the decisions it made earlier in the session"],c:[1],e:"Persisting decisions where they can be re-read keeps them authoritative, rather than relying on them surviving in a long conversation.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The team wants Claude Code to reach an internal service. What is the appropriate mechanism?",o:["A prompt describing the service's API in sufficient detail","A Skill containing instructions for calling the service by hand","Configuration listing the service's endpoints for reference","An MCP server exposing that service's operations as tools"],c:[3],e:"MCP is the standard integration path for reaching an external system; Skills and prompts describe procedure rather than providing access.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team judge whether a change to their shared configuration helped?",o:["By asking engineers whether the change felt like an improvement","By counting how many review comments were raised the following week","By running a fixed set of representative tasks before and after","By checking whether the configuration file is shorter than before"],c:[2],e:"A controlled before-and-after on representative tasks is what makes the comparison meaningful; perception and incidental counts are confounded.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"What is the risk of putting task-specific procedures into project-wide configuration?",o:["The configuration file will exceed the maximum length that is permitted","Task procedures cannot be expressed in configuration file format","Project configuration is not read when a task-specific Skill exists","They apply to work they were never meant for, adding irrelevant context"],c:[3],e:"Universal placement means universal application. Specific procedures belong in Skills so they load only when relevant.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants Claude Code to run a multi-step task without confirming each edit. What should they consider?",o:["Whether the repository has been fully backed up before the run is started","Whether the permission bounds configured still cover the risk of those edits","Whether the task will complete faster than it would do interactively","Whether the model tier that was selected supports autonomous operation"],c:[1],e:"Reducing per-step confirmation still operates inside configured permissions, so the question is whether those bounds are appropriate for the work.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should large generated diffs be reviewed in smaller pieces?",o:["Version control systems reject any diff above a certain size threshold","Large diffs cannot be displayed correctly by most review tools","The model produces lower-quality code later in a long generation","Reviewer attention degrades over a long diff, so errors slip through"],c:[3],e:"The constraint is human review capacity. Breaking work into reviewable units is what keeps the check meaningful.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"m",q:"Which two make a tool set easier for Claude Code to use correctly? (Select 2)",o:["Each tool's description states explicitly when not to use it","Tool names follow the internal service naming convention","Every available tool is exposed so nothing is ever unavailable","The set is scoped to the tools the current task actually needs"],c:[0,3],e:"Explicit boundaries and a scoped set both improve selection accuracy; exposing everything degrades it, and naming conventions are neutral.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A generated test suite passes but does not actually exercise the edge cases. What does this indicate?",o:["The model tier being used is insufficient for test generation work","The existing code is untestable in its current structure","The request specified tests but not what they needed to cover","Generated tests are inherently unable to cover edge cases"],c:[2],e:"Coverage is a requirement that has to be stated. Asking for tests without specifying what they must exercise leaves the selection to the model.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Where should a developer's personal preferences, such as their preferred commit message style, be configured?",o:["In the repository README, where any other developer is able to read it","At project level, so that the entire team benefits from the convention","At user level, where it applies to their own work without affecting others","In a Skill, so that it can be invoked whenever a commit is being made"],c:[2],e:"Personal preference belongs at user level precisely because it should not be imposed on the team through shared configuration.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A subagent is given a research subtask but produces work unrelated to the wider question. What is the likely cause?",o:["The subagent inherited the coordinator's context and became confused","The subagent's tools were too narrowly scoped for the subtask","The subagent used a different model tier from the coordinator","The coordinator did not pass enough context in the subagent's brief"],c:[3],e:"Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's conversation, so everything they need must be passed explicitly in their prompt.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The final report omits an entire topic area the question required. Where does the fault lie?",o:["In the source material, which did not contain that topic area","In the subagents, which failed to cover the material they were given","In the coordinator's decomposition, which never created that subtask","In the synthesis step, which dropped the section when combining"],c:[2],e:"A missing topic area means the work was never delegated, which is a decomposition failure at the coordinator rather than a subagent one.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"How should the coordinator launch three independent research subagents efficiently?",o:["Launch the first and let it spawn the other two in turn","Combine the three subtasks into one larger subagent brief","Emit the three delegation calls together in one response","Launch them one at a time, waiting for each to return"],c:[2],e:"Issuing multiple delegation calls in a single response runs them in parallel and avoids serial round-trip latency.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Can two subagents exchange findings directly with one another?",o:["No, unless the coordinator has finished and released control of the run","No — communication routes through the coordinator that dispatched them","Yes, if they are configured to share a common context window","Yes, provided they were launched within the same parallel batch"],c:[1],e:"Hub-and-spoke orchestration routes everything through the coordinator; subagents have no direct channel between them.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A subagent's exploration produced many dead ends. What should be returned to the coordinator?",o:["The findings and their sources, not the full exploration trail","The complete working context so the coordinator can audit it","Only a numeric confidence score for the subtask's conclusion","The raw text of every source the subagent opened while working"],c:[0],e:"Returning the distilled result is the point of isolation; passing back the whole trail reintroduces the noise it was meant to contain.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The report contains a claim no one can trace to a source. What design change prevents this?",o:["Run the synthesis step twice and keep only the claims in both","Ask the coordinator to verify every claim during synthesis","Instruct the model not to include unsourced claims in the report","Require subagents to return each finding with its source attached"],c:[3],e:"Attribution has to be carried from where the finding was made. Asking synthesis to verify after the fact cannot recover a source that was never captured.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"How many tools should each research subagent be given?",o:["As many as the coordinator itself has been given","All available tools, so it is never blocked mid-task","A small set relevant to its role, typically a handful","Exactly one, so its behaviour stays entirely predictable"],c:[2],e:"Scoping each subagent to the few tools its role needs preserves selection reliability; a large set degrades it.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A synthesis agent has been given web search tools. What is the concern?",o:["Its role is to integrate findings, so search widens its remit unnecessarily","Two separate agents searching the same sources duplicates the work","Search results cannot be combined with findings returned by subagents","A synthesis agent is not permitted to call any tools of any kind at all"],c:[0],e:"Tools should match role. Giving the synthesiser search capability blurs responsibility and invites it to redo the researchers' work.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A subagent fails partway through its subtask. What should the coordinator receive?",o:["Nothing, so the coordinator retries the subtask from the beginning","The full stack trace from the underlying tool that failed","The failure type, what was attempted, and any partial findings","A generic error so the coordinator can decide how to proceed"],c:[2],e:"Rich failure propagation lets the coordinator decide intelligently; silence forces blind retries and a bare error discards recoverable work.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"When is a multi-agent design the wrong choice for a research question?",o:["When the research is expected to take longer than a few minutes","When the relevant sources are spread across many different systems","When the answer needs to be traceable back to its evidence","When the question is narrow enough for one agent to answer directly"],c:[3],e:"Orchestration overhead is only justified by genuinely separable work; a narrow question doesn't benefit from being split.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the quality of the research system be evaluated?",o:["By whether the finished report reads as fluent and well organised throughout","By how long a complete research run takes from start to finish","Against questions with known good answers, scored on coverage and accuracy","By the number of distinct sources each run manages to consult"],c:[2],e:"Known-answer questions scored on substantive criteria measure quality; source counts, runtime, and fluency are proxies that can all improve while accuracy falls.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Findings from six subagents exceed what fits comfortably in the synthesis prompt. What is the sound approach?",o:["Move to a model with a larger context window and pass everything","Synthesise in pairs, combining two subagents' findings at a time","Have subagents return structured summaries sized for synthesis","Drop the findings from whichever subagents returned the least"],c:[2],e:"Designing the return format to fit the synthesis step is the structural fix; simply enlarging the window defers the same problem.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A subagent needs access to an internal document store. What is the appropriate integration?",o:["An MCP server exposing search and retrieval over that store","A tool that returns the store's URL for the model to visit","Pasting the store's contents into the subagent's prompt","A Skill describing how the document store is organised"],c:[0],e:"MCP provides the standard, reusable access path to an external system, which pasting content and describing structure do not.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What determines when the coordinator's own loop should stop?",o:["Whether a fixed maximum number of subagents has been reached","Whether the finished report has reached a target length measured in words","Whether the elapsed time has passed a configured threshold","Whether every delegated subtask has returned and synthesis is complete"],c:[3],e:"Completion is defined by the work being done, with caps and timeouts acting as safety nets rather than the primary condition.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The system produces good reports on technical questions and poor ones on policy questions. What should be investigated?",o:["Whether the model tier is adequate for policy subject matter","Whether the report template needs a different structure for policy","Whether decomposition and source selection suit that question type","Whether policy questions are inherently unsuited to the system"],c:[2],e:"A systematic difference by question type points at how those questions are broken down and which sources are consulted.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"m",q:"Which three should be passed explicitly in a subagent's brief? (Select 3)",o:["Any constraints on which sources it should treat as authoritative","The specific question the subagent is responsible for answering","The format its findings must come back in","The coordinator's full conversation history to date"],c:[0,1,2],e:"Subagents need their own question, a defined return format, and any source constraints; passing the coordinator's full history reintroduces the noise isolation removes.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Two subagents return contradictory findings on the same point. What should the coordinator do?",o:["Accept whichever finding came from the subagent that ran longer","Discard both of the findings and omit the point from the final report","Surface the disagreement and the evidence rather than silently choosing","Re-run both subagents until the two findings come into agreement"],c:[2],e:"A genuine evidential conflict is information. Concealing it by arbitrary selection or omission misrepresents the state of the research.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"What is the most useful thing to log from a research run?",o:["Which subtasks were created, what each returned","The wall-clock duration of each individual subagent's work","The total number of tokens consumed across the whole run","The final report only, since intermediate steps are transient"],c:[0],e:"Logging the decomposition, returns, and sources is what makes a run diagnosable when the report turns out to be wrong.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The coordinator delegates a subtask that is itself highly complex. What is a reasonable design response?",o:["Split it arbitrarily into equal parts spread across several subagents","Increase the subagent's tool allocation in order to compensate for it","Let that subagent decompose further rather than forcing a flat structure","Handle it in the coordinator directly rather than delegating it out"],c:[2],e:"Nested decomposition matches structure to the problem; arbitrary splitting cuts across natural boundaries and extra tools don't address complexity.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the coordinator avoid holding every subagent's raw output in its context?",o:["The coordinator is not permitted to read subagent working notes","Raw output invalidates the coordinator's prompt cache each turn","Raw output cannot be interpreted correctly by the coordinator","It crowds out the synthesis task with material already distilled"],c:[3],e:"The coordinator's job is synthesis, and filling its context with undistilled working material competes directly with that.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Different teams need access to different internal systems. How should this be handled?",o:["Expose every MCP server and rely on teams to use the right one","Build one merged server exposing all systems behind a single tool","Scope each team's tool access to the systems their role requires","Grant full access and audit usage after the fact each month"],c:[2],e:"Least-privilege scoping is both a security control and a reliability one, since a smaller relevant tool set improves selection accuracy.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Before building a custom MCP server for an internal wiki, what should be checked?",o:["Whether the team has capacity to maintain a new server","Which transport the new custom server would need to be built on","Whether the wiki's data volume fits within a context window","Whether a community or existing server already covers that system"],c:[3],e:"Evaluating existing servers first avoids building and maintaining an integration that may already exist.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks a question that spans three connected systems. What makes this work reliably?",o:["The engineer states which system should be queried in their question","All three systems are merged behind one tool with a mode parameter","The agent queries all three systems and combines whatever returns","Each system's tools are clearly described so the model routes correctly"],c:[3],e:"Clear, distinct descriptions are what let the model route across systems without the user having to direct it manually.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tool explores a large unfamiliar codebase and runs out of context before finishing. What is the design fix?",o:["Explore progressively, loading only what each step requires","Restrict the tool to repositories below a fixed size","Switch to the model with the largest available context window","Load the entire codebase at the start of every session"],c:[0],e:"Progressive discovery keeps context proportional to the question, whereas monolithic loading exhausts it regardless of window size.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Answers about a legacy service are confidently wrong because its documentation is outdated. What addresses this?",o:["Ask engineers to update the documentation before using the tool","Remove the documentation so only the code is ever consulted","Instruct the model to treat all documentation as unreliable","Ground answers in the code itself and flag where docs disagree"],c:[3],e:"Preferring authoritative sources while surfacing disagreement gives engineers the real picture, rather than hiding the conflict.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A tool that queries a production database is being added. What control matters most?",o:["Logging every query it runs for review by the team afterwards","Restricting it to read operations that cannot alter production data","Ensuring its description warns the model to be careful with it","Limiting the number of times it may be called within a single session"],c:[1],e:"A technical restriction to read-only is enforceable; warnings, logging, and call limits don't prevent a destructive statement executing.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tool should answer from internal systems only, but sometimes answers from general knowledge. What is the fix?",o:["Ask engineers to phrase questions so that only internal data applies","Reduce the model tier so that it relies rather less on its own knowledge","Instruct it to answer only from retrieved results and say when it cannot","Connect more internal systems so that more questions can be answered"],c:[2],e:"Bounding the model to retrieved content with an explicit fallback is the direct control on ungrounded answers.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should retrieved results include where they came from?",o:["Sources are required before results can be cached for reuse","Retrieval fails silently unless each result carries a source","Engineers can verify the answer against the system it came from","Sources reduce the number of tokens that each result consumes"],c:[2],e:"Traceability is what makes an answer checkable, which matters most where the tool is being trusted about unfamiliar systems.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team measure whether the tool is genuinely useful?",o:["How many internal systems the tool has been connected to in total","How many queries the tool handles across the organisation","Whether engineers reach correct answers faster than without it","How positively engineers rate the tool in a survey"],c:[2],e:"Time to a correct answer is the outcome the tool exists to improve; usage, coverage, and sentiment can all rise without it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An MCP server exposes eighteen tools and routing accuracy has fallen. What is the appropriate response?",o:["Move to a more capable model that handles larger tool sets","Split it so each agent sees only the tools relevant to its role","Rename the tools so their names are more distinctive","Add a routing instruction to the system prompt listing all eighteen"],c:[1],e:"Reducing the number of options each agent faces is the reliable fix; naming and prompt-level routing rules scale poorly.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long exploration session has accumulated many file contents no longer relevant. What helps?",o:["Summarising all of the file contents down into a single block","Clearing superseded results from context as the task moves on","Asking the model to ignore the files it no longer needs","Starting a new session whenever the context becomes large"],c:[1],e:"Actively removing superseded content is more precise than summarising everything or discarding the whole session.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Engineers want the tool to follow their team's architectural conventions. Where should those live?",o:["In each engineer's individual question when they ask it","In configuration the tool reads for that team's repositories","In a Skill invoked manually at the start of each session","In the MCP server's tool descriptions written for that team"],c:[1],e:"Team conventions applying to all work in a repository belong in shared configuration rather than being restated or hidden in tool metadata.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tool is asked a question requiring a change to be made rather than information. What should happen?",o:["It should be out of scope unless change tooling was deliberately granted","The tool should attempt the change and then report back on what it did","The tool should describe the change for the engineer to apply","The tool should escalate the request to a senior engineer"],c:[0],e:"A read-oriented tool shouldn't acquire write capability implicitly; extending scope is a deliberate design decision with its own controls.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Answers about one particular system are consistently poor. What should be examined?",o:["Whether the model tier needs raising for that system only","Whether engineers happen to ask worse questions about that system","Whether that system is inherently too complex to answer about","Whether retrieval over that system is returning the right material"],c:[3],e:"A system-specific failure points at that system's integration and retrieval quality rather than at the model or the users.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"m",q:"Which two belong in an MCP tool's description? (Select 2)",o:["The programming language the server was implemented in","How it differs from a similar tool on another system","The kinds of question it is designed to answer","The internal team that maintains the underlying service"],c:[1,2],e:"Purpose and explicit boundaries against similar tools drive correct selection; ownership and implementation detail don't.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks a question whose answer depends on which environment they mean. What should the tool do?",o:["Answer for production, since that is the most commonly meant","Answer for every environment and let the engineer select","Answer generally without reference to any specific environment","Ask which environment before answering"],c:[3],e:"A material ambiguity should be resolved rather than guessed, since a confidently wrong environment-specific answer is worse than a question.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What is the risk of the tool caching answers about internal systems?",o:["Cached answers cannot be attributed to a specific engineer","Systems change, so a cached answer can become quietly wrong","Cached answers consume more context than fresh retrieval","Caching prevents the tool from citing its sources correctly"],c:[1],e:"Staleness is the real hazard when caching answers about systems that evolve independently of the cache.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team decide which internal system to integrate next?",o:["By which systems have the best existing documentation","By which team has asked most recently for an integration","By which systems engineers most often need answers about","By which systems are easiest to build a connector for"],c:[2],e:"Demand for answers is the value signal; ease of integration and documentation quality affect cost, not benefit.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why is progressive discovery preferable to loading a whole system's context upfront?",o:["Only material relevant to the question competes for the model's attention","Progressive discovery produces answers in fewer total tool calls","Loading everything upfront is not technically possible for large systems","Loading everything upfront prevents the model from citing its sources"],c:[0],e:"Keeping irrelevant material out of context is the substantive benefit; it usually costs more tool calls, not fewer.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A team wants their MCP server reachable by tooling on several machines. Which transport applies?",o:["Neither, since an MCP server must run on the machine that calls it","Either one, since both transports support remote and local use equally","Streamable HTTP, for a server running as an independent remote process","stdio, which runs the server as a local subprocess of the client"],c:[2],e:"stdio is for a local subprocess; reaching a server from several machines is what Streamable HTTP is for.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The pipeline job hangs indefinitely when Claude Code runs. What is the most likely cause?",o:["The model tier selected is not available to automated pipelines","The repository is too large to be analysed within the job timeout","The pipeline lacks the permission needed to read the repository contents","It is running interactively and waiting for input that never arrives"],c:[3],e:"Non-interactive execution has to be requested explicitly; without it the process waits for input a pipeline can never supply.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Downstream pipeline steps need to parse the review output. What should be requested?",o:["Whatever format the model considers clearest for a human reader","Output written directly into the pull request as review comments","Plain prose, which downstream steps can search with pattern matching","A defined structured output format the next step can consume"],c:[3],e:"Machine-consumed output needs a defined structure; parsing prose with pattern matching is fragile and breaks on ordinary variation.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The review step should run on every pull request and developers wait on it. Which processing mode applies?",o:["Batch processing, since pull requests arrive in high volume","Batch processing, since it halves the cost of every review","Synchronous calls, since the result is blocking a merge","Whichever mode has the most capacity available at the time"],c:[2],e:"A blocking pre-merge check cannot use asynchronous batch processing; the cost saving is irrelevant if the result arrives too late.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The nightly release-notes job is latency-tolerant and high volume. What suits it?",o:["Streaming, so progress is visible while the job is running","Batch processing, which trades immediacy for substantially lower cost","Whichever processing mode the pre-merge review step already makes use of","Synchronous calls, to keep the job's implementation simple"],c:[1],e:"An overnight, non-blocking workload is exactly the batch case, where the latency tradeoff costs nothing.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The review flags too many trivial issues and engineers are ignoring it. What is the fix?",o:["Move to a more capable model tier to improve its judgement","Define categorically what counts as an issue worth raising","Ask the model to raise only issues it is highly confident about","Limit the review to a maximum number of issues per pull request"],c:[1],e:"Categorical criteria are actionable where confidence thresholds are not, and capping the count hides issues rather than improving relevance.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A tool call inside the pipeline fails. What should the job do?",o:["Retry indefinitely until the underlying tool becomes available","Continue on silently so that the pipeline is not blocked by the error","Mark the pull request as approved so that merges are not delayed","Report the failure clearly and exit in a way the pipeline can act on"],c:[3],e:"An unattended job must fail visibly. Silent continuation and auto-approval both let a broken check pass as a working one.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The pipeline needs the same review standards the team uses locally. How is that achieved?",o:["By having a senior engineer review the pipeline's output weekly","By reading the project configuration committed to the repository","By duplicating the standards into the pipeline's own configuration","By passing the standards as a long prompt in the pipeline script"],c:[1],e:"Project configuration in the repository is the single source of truth, so both local and pipeline runs pick up the same standards.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team tell whether the automated review is actually catching real problems?",o:["Count how many issues it raises over the course of a typical week","Ask engineers whether they find the automated review helpful overall","Compare its findings against issues later found in production or review","Check that it raises at least one issue on every pull request"],c:[2],e:"Validating against problems that genuinely mattered is the real test; volume and sentiment can both look healthy while the check misses what counts.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The build triage step should investigate failures but never change code. How is that enforced?",o:["By granting it only read and analysis tools","By running it against a copy of the repository instead","By instructing it in the prompt not to modify any files","By reviewing its output before any changes are applied"],c:[0],e:"Capability boundaries are enforced by what tools exist, not by instructions asking the model to refrain.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Review quality varies between pull requests of similar size. What should be examined first?",o:["Whether the model is inherently inconsistent when reviewing code","Whether different engineers on the team write differently structured code","Whether the diff and its surrounding context are being supplied consistently","Whether pull requests are submitted at different times of day"],c:[2],e:"Inconsistent inputs are the usual driver of inconsistent output, so what actually reaches the model is the first thing to check.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"What should the pipeline do when Claude Code's output cannot be parsed?",o:["Pass the unparsed output to the next step for it to interpret","Skip the step and continue the pipeline as though it passed","Fail the step explicitly rather than proceeding on an assumption","Retry once with the same input and continue regardless of result"],c:[2],e:"An unparseable result is a failure and should surface as one; continuing pretends a check ran when it did not.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated release notes occasionally invent a change that was not in the diff. What reduces this?",o:["Grounding the notes strictly in the commit and diff content supplied","Adding a disclaimer noting that the release notes are generated automatically","Generating the notes twice and keeping entries present in both","Asking for shorter release notes with fewer individual entries"],c:[0],e:"Constraining generation to the supplied source is the direct control; length limits and duplicate runs don't prevent invention.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The triage step needs to read build logs from an external system. What is the appropriate integration?",o:["Copying logs into the repository so they are available locally","Giving the step shell access to fetch the logs however it can","Pasting the full logs into the prompt on every pipeline run","An MCP server exposing log retrieval as a scoped tool"],c:[3],e:"A scoped MCP tool is the maintainable path; broad shell access over-grants capability and pasting full logs wastes context.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the pipeline log the exact prompt and output for each run?",o:["So the pipeline can detect when the model has been updated","So a bad review can be diagnosed after the fact","So token usage can be attributed to individual engineers","So the same prompt can be reused without regenerating it"],c:[1],e:"Reproducibility for diagnosis is the substantive reason; the other uses are incidental and don't require full logging.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"m",q:"Which two are required for Claude Code to run correctly inside a pipeline? (Select 2)",o:["The most capable model tier, regardless of the task's difficulty","A defined output format the next pipeline step can consume","Non-interactive execution so it never waits for terminal input","A human reviewer available to approve each run before it proceeds"],c:[1,2],e:"Non-interactive execution and machine-readable output are the structural requirements; tier and human approval are separate decisions.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The review step's cost has grown as the team has. What is the most promising optimisation?",o:["Reviewing only the changed files rather than the whole repository","Moving the review to run overnight instead of on each request","Running the review on every second pull request instead of all","Shortening the review output so that fewer tokens are generated"],c:[0],e:"Scoping input to the diff cuts cost without weakening the check; skipping requests and deferring it both reduce its value.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Different repositories need different review standards. How should this be structured?",o:["One shared configuration lists the rules for every repository","Each repository carries its own project-level configuration","Each engineer configures standards for the repositories they own","The pipeline branches on repository name to select a prompt"],c:[1],e:"Configuration living with the repository keeps standards local to the code they govern and travels correctly as repositories change.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A very large pull request exceeds what fits in one review request. What is the sound approach?",o:["Review only the first portion that fits within the limit","Reject the pull request and ask for it to be split up","Review it in coherent chunks and aggregate the findings","Summarise the diff and review the summary instead"],c:[2],e:"Chunked review with aggregation preserves coverage; truncating loses it, and reviewing a summary reviews the wrong artefact.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What should happen if the review step is unavailable when a pull request is opened?",o:["The pull request is blocked until the step becomes available again","The team decides in advance whether that blocks or warns","The step is retried silently until it eventually succeeds","The pull request proceeds, since the step is only advisory anyway"],c:[1],e:"Whether an unavailable check blocks or warns is a deliberate policy decision that should be made and encoded, not defaulted to.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Why is a schema on the review output not sufficient to guarantee its correctness?",o:["Schemas cannot express the fields a code review needs","Schemas are only applied after the output has been generated","Schema validation is not available in non-interactive runs","A schema constrains structure"],c:[3],e:"Structural validity and substantive correctness are different properties; a well-formed review can still be wrong about the code.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A schema is applied to extracted invoice data and errors still reach the database. Why?",o:["A schema enforces structure, not whether the values are correct","The schema was applied after the data had already been written","Schema validation only applies to documents above a size threshold","Schemas cannot express the field types an invoice requires"],c:[0],e:"Schemas eliminate syntax errors but not semantic ones — wrong values, mismatched sums, and fabricated fields all pass a valid schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Some contracts omit a field the schema marks as required. What is the consequence?",o:["The field is populated with an empty string by the validator","The model is pushed to invent a value to satisfy the requirement","The extraction fails and the document is skipped automatically","The schema is relaxed automatically for that document only"],c:[1],e:"Making a field required when the source may not contain it invites fabrication; nullable fields are what allow honest absence.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Line items extract correctly but the totals do not reconcile. What check catches this?",o:["A stricter schema that requires the total to be a numeric field","A second extraction run compared against the first for agreement","A confidence score requested from the model for the total field","An arithmetic validation comparing the total against the line items"],c:[3],e:"A semantic consistency check on the relationship between fields is what catches this; type constraints and repeat runs do not.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Extraction is performed through a tool with a JSON schema. What does this reliably prevent?",o:["Malformed output that downstream parsing would reject","Totals that fail to match the line items they summarise","Values that are plausible but not present in the document","Fields populated from the wrong section of the document"],c:[0],e:"Tool schemas guarantee well-formed structure. Every remaining failure listed is semantic and needs separate validation.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Scanned forms of poor quality produce unreliable extractions. What is the sound design response?",o:["Extract anyway and correct the errors when they are noticed downstream","Reject the documents outright and ask the sender to resubmit all of them","Use a more capable model tier on every document in order to compensate","Route low-quality documents to human review rather than extracting blindly"],c:[3],e:"Detecting the condition and routing it differently is what protects the database, since blind extraction pushes the cost downstream.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Should extraction be built as an agent or a fixed workflow?",o:["An agent, since documents vary in quality and completeness","An agent, so it can decide which fields matter per document","Either, since the two approaches perform identically here","A workflow, since the steps are known and repeat identically"],c:[3],e:"Known, repeatable steps favour a workflow. Input variation is handled by validation and routing rather than by dynamic control flow.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the extraction system be evaluated before it goes live?",o:["Against documents with known correct values across the quality range","By confirming it processes documents faster than manual entry","By checking that every output validates against the schema","By reviewing a sample of the outputs for their general plausibility"],c:[0],e:"Known-correct ground truth across representative quality is what measures accuracy; schema validity and plausibility miss semantic errors.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A field is sometimes extracted from the wrong section of a long contract. What helps most?",o:["Instructing where in the document the field is expected to appear","Shortening the contract before it is passed through for extraction","Requesting a confidence score alongside the extracted value","Extracting the field twice and comparing the two results"],c:[0],e:"Locational guidance disambiguates similar-looking content; repeat extraction can make the same mistake twice.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Extraction volume is high and results are needed by the next morning. What suits this?",o:["Batch processing, which fits high volume with tolerant latency","Streaming, so partial results are available during the run","Synchronous calls, so failures are detected as they happen","Whichever mode the interactive review interface already uses"],c:[0],e:"High volume with an overnight deadline is the canonical batch case, where the asynchronous tradeoff costs nothing.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the extracted output record which document each value came from?",o:["So a wrong value in the database can be traced back and corrected","So the model can be prevented from reprocessing old documents","So documents can be deleted once extraction has completed","So that the same document is never processed more than one time"],c:[0],e:"Provenance is what makes downstream errors correctable, which matters when incorrect data is expensive to unwind.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A field extracts correctly 97% of the time. What determines whether that is acceptable?",o:["Whether a more capable model tier could raise it above 99% accuracy","Whether 97% exceeds the accuracy of the manual process","Whether the failures are spread evenly across document types","What the 3% cost to detect and correct once they reach the database"],c:[3],e:"The cost of the residual errors is what decides acceptability, particularly where wrong data is expensive to unwind.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Where should a human review step sit in the extraction pipeline?",o:["After the write, so errors can be corrected in the database","Before extraction, to check that documents are worth processing","After validation and before anything is written to the database","At the end of each day, reviewing everything processed since"],c:[2],e:"Review belongs immediately before the irreversible step, which is the point at which correction becomes expensive.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The database write should be a tool the model calls directly. What is the concern?",o:["Tools cannot be used to write into an external database at all","An incorrect extraction becomes an irreversible write with no gate","Database writes are too slow to be performed within a tool call","The model cannot construct valid database statements reliably"],c:[1],e:"Letting the model write directly removes the validation gate before an expensive-to-reverse action.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Documents arrive in several languages. What should the design account for?",o:["That each language requires a separate model to be selected","That field labels differ, so location-based rules may not transfer","That non-English documents must be translated before extraction","That extraction accuracy is identical regardless of language"],c:[1],e:"Language changes how fields are labelled and laid out, which is what breaks rules tuned to one language's documents.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"m",q:"Which two checks catch errors a schema cannot? (Select 2)",o:["Confirming the output parses as valid JSON","Confirming extracted values actually appear in the source document","Confirming every field is present and correctly typed","Verifying that totals reconcile with their component values"],c:[1,3],e:"Reconciliation and source verification are semantic checks; presence, typing, and parseability are exactly what a schema already covers.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An extraction returns a plausible supplier name that does not appear in the document. What is this?",o:["A formatting error in the way that the field was returned","A retrieval failure caused by poor document quality","A schema violation that validation should have rejected","A fabricated value, which schema validation will not detect"],c:[3],e:"An invented but well-formed value passes validation cleanly, which is why source verification is needed alongside the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Extraction and validation currently run as one combined step. What is the argument for separating them?",o:["Separate steps run faster than a single combined one","Combined steps are not supported by the Messages API","Validation cannot be expressed within an extraction prompt","Failures become attributable to extraction or to validation"],c:[3],e:"Separation makes failure attributable, which is what allows the right component to be fixed.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team handle a document type the system has never seen?",o:["Process it normally, since the schema will catch any problems","Process it with a more capable model tier as a precaution","Route it for human handling until it has been evaluated","Reject it automatically as an unsupported document type"],c:[2],e:"Unvalidated document types shouldn't flow into an expensive-to-unwind database on the assumption that validation will catch everything.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the system record the confidence-relevant conditions of each extraction, such as scan quality?",o:["It allows later errors to be correlated with the conditions that produced them","It reduces the number of documents that end up needing human review","It allows the model to improve its own accuracy on future documents","It is required before any extracted data may be written to the database at all"],c:[0],e:"Recording conditions makes patterns in the failures visible, which is what lets the routing rules be improved.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"What should the extraction tool's schema do about a field that is genuinely optional?",o:["Mark it required and accept an empty string when it is missing","Omit it from the schema and extract it in a separate step","Mark it nullable so absence can be reported honestly","Mark it required and let validation reject documents lacking it"],c:[2],e:"Nullable fields let the model report genuine absence rather than being pushed into inventing a value to satisfy the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  ]
});
